    13	# Design analysis for the fused variant
    14	# -------------------------------------
    15	# 1. Concrete fused behavior:
    16	#    - The original task had a single wall color and an undirected flood-fill.
    17	#    - Here the 3x3 green/red target is still surrounded by a frame, but the frame
    18	#      now mixes two wall colors:
    19	#      * magenta(6): fully solid wall
    20	#      * blue(1): directional gate wall
    21	#    - Blue cells are only placed on rays extending straight out from the red
    22	#      center. They can be traversed only along that ray and only from the side
    23	#      nearer the center toward the side farther from the center. Sideways exits
    24	#      from a blue cell are forbidden.
    25	#    - Example: if a blue chain goes upward from the target, a maroon sitting in a
    26	#      one-cell side pocket touching that chain is visually adjacent to the chain
    27	#      but is still unreachable, because the path would need a sideways move out of
    28	#      blue. A maroon beyond the tip of the chain is reachable, because the motion
    29	#      stays radial until it exits to orange floor.
    30	#
    31	# 2. Complexity calibration:
    32	#    - Only two wall colors are used, with one repeated blue behavior everywhere.
    33	#      This is enough to be visible without introducing too many mappings.
    34	#    - Blue is restricted to the center row/column rays so the direction rule is
    35	#      readable from geometry alone; we avoid arbitrary per-cell directions.
    36	#
    37	# 3. Broken original assumptions and solver changes:
    38	#    - Original assumption: every non-wall non-target color next to the destination
    39	#      is an ordinary passable start cell. Not true anymore: a blue neighbor can be
    40	#      entered only from the inner side of its ray.
    41	#    - Original assumption: passability is symmetric. Not true anymore: blue edges
    42	#      are directed and do not allow reverse or sideways traversal.
    43	#    - Original assumption: the wall ring still contains ordinary floor cells from
    44	#      which floor color can be inferred. Not true anymore: the ring can be all
    45	#      magenta/blue, so color inference from the ring is removed and the known
    46	#      palette is used directly.
    47	#
    48	# 4. Placement legality and visual cleanliness:
    49	#    - Blue chains stay on the center row/column, so they never overlap each other.
    50	#    - Each side pocket is a single floor cell with its other neighbors turned into
    51	#      magenta, guaranteeing it is a clean "false branch" and not an accidental
    52	#      alternate corridor.
    53	#    - Pockets are placed only beyond the first blue gate cell so they never touch
    54	#      the 3x3 target block.
    55	#
    56	# 5. Non-degeneracy target:
